   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Awesome, thanks Noam! Any idea on how to get a lagged value that is not a state variable or a derivative? I have another variable in the model that I&#39;m trying to compare between time steps. Thanks!</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Monday, December 22, 2014 7:58:53 AM UTC-8, Alison Rachel Marklein wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hey everyone,<div><br><div>I am building a semi-discrete model and need to access time t-1 in my ode model. I have tried using a counter and an outside vector, but the variable at t-1 is always NA or 0. Has anyone else run into this problem, or been able to access previous values in a differential equation model? Thanks for the help!</div><div><br></div><div>Best, </div><div>Alison</div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "PoAbxof5Ef4J",
   "from": "Noam Ross",
   "author": "8c8f0ca54c4a2459",
   "date": "2014-12-22T18:39:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: [D-RUG]: Re: Accessing t-1 in ode model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"font-size:1em;font-family:Helvetica,arial,freesans,clean,sans-serif;color:rgb(34,34,34);border:none;line-height:1.2;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><p style=\"margin:1em 0px\">I think you will probably have to re-calculate the intermediate value you describe if you need both its current and its lagged value in order to calculate the next derivative.  I don’t believe either <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:nowrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248)\">lagvalue()</code> or  <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:nowrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248)\">lagderiv()</code> can provide intermediate values that you may return from your derivative function (e.g., “inter*” in <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:nowrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248)\">return(list(c(dx, dy), c(inter1, inter2)</code>).</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1em 0px\">Depending on your exact application, you might not have to calculate it twice, but I’d need to see the code/problem to see. </p>\r\n<div title=\"MDH:SSB0aGluayB5b3Ugd2lsbCBwcm9iYWJseSBoYXZlIHRvIHJlLWNhbGN1bGF0ZSB0aGUgaW50ZXJt\r\nZWRpYXRlIHZhbHVlIHlvdSBkZXNjcmliZSBpZiB5b3UgbmVlZCBib3RoIGl0cyBjdXJyZW50IGFu\r\nZCBpdHMgbGFnZ2VkIHZhbHVlIGluIG9yZGVyIHRvIGNhbGN1bGF0ZSB0aGUgbmV4dCBkZXJpdmF0\r\naXZlLiDCoEkgZG9uJ3QgYmVsaWV2ZSBlaXRoZXIgYGxhZ3ZhbHVlKClgIG9yIMKgYGxhZ2Rlcml2\r\nKClgIGNhbiBwcm92aWRlIGludGVybWVkaWF0ZSB2YWx1ZXMgdGhhdCB5b3UgbWF5IHJldHVybiBm\r\ncm9tIHlvdXIgZGVyaXZhdGl2ZSBmdW5jdGlvbiAoZS5nLiwgImludGVyKiIgaW4gYHJldHVybihs\r\naXN0KGMoZHgsIGR5KSwgYyhpbn